1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,510
Milk sets into curd overnight.

2
00:00:02,510 --> 00:00:04,680
Tiny cells live in hot springs.

3
00:00:04,680 --> 00:00:07,211
Green scum spreads over a dirty pond.

4
00:00:07,611 --> 00:00:09,799
One kingdom is behind all three.

5
00:00:10,249 --> 00:00:14,100
Diya left warm milk with a spoon
of curd on the kitchen shelf.

6
00:00:14,100 --> 00:00:16,403
By morning, it was thick and sour.

7
00:00:16,403 --> 00:00:17,652
Who did the work?

8
00:00:18,002 --> 00:00:18,902
Bacteria.

9
00:00:18,832 --> 00:00:21,581
Living cells, far too small to see.

10
00:00:22,031 --> 00:00:26,550
By the end, you will know the kingdom
of bacteria, from curd makers to

11
00:00:26,550 --> 00:00:27,702
nitrogen fixers.

12
00:00:28,352 --> 00:00:30,492
Let's zoom into one spoon of curd.

13
00:00:30,842 --> 00:00:32,035
Here is the curd.

14
00:00:32,035 --> 00:00:34,048
Thick, white and a little sour.

15
00:00:34,548 --> 00:00:35,448
Zoom in.

16
00:00:35,333 --> 00:00:38,943
Between the milk fat droplets,
tiny rod shaped cells.

17
00:00:38,943 --> 00:00:40,350
These are bacteria.

18
00:00:40,850 --> 00:00:42,897
Zoom into a typical bacterium.

19
00:00:42,897 --> 00:00:44,895
Outside, a rigid cell wall.

20
00:00:44,895 --> 00:00:47,302
Just inside it, the cell membrane.

21
00:00:47,802 --> 00:00:50,081
Its DNA lies loose in the cell.

22
00:00:50,081 --> 00:00:52,134
There is no nuclear membrane.

23
00:00:52,134 --> 00:00:56,724
Tiny ribosomes make its proteins,
and a flagellum can push it along.

24
00:00:57,374 --> 00:01:00,341
Bacteria are the only
members of kingdom Monera.

25
00:01:00,691 --> 00:01:05,411
They are the most abundant microorganisms,
and live almost everywhere.

26
00:01:05,411 --> 00:01:08,614
Hot springs, deserts,
snow and deep oceans.

27
00:01:09,064 --> 00:01:11,149
They come in four main shapes.

28
00:01:11,149 --> 00:01:12,670
Round ones are cocci.

29
00:01:12,670 --> 00:01:14,597
Rod shaped ones are bacilli.

30
00:01:15,047 --> 00:01:17,111
Comma shaped ones are vibrio.

31
00:01:17,111 --> 00:01:18,906
Spiral ones are spirilla.

32
00:01:19,556 --> 00:01:21,633
Monera has two big groups.

33
00:01:21,633 --> 00:01:23,799
Archaebacteria and eubacteria.

34
00:01:24,149 --> 00:01:27,702
Archaebacteria live where
almost nothing else can.

35
00:01:27,702 --> 00:01:30,459
Halophiles live in very salty places.

36
00:01:30,459 --> 00:01:33,205
Thermoacidophiles live in hot springs.

37
00:01:33,205 --> 00:01:35,727
Methanogens live in marshy places.

38
00:01:36,477 --> 00:01:41,291
Methanogens also live in the gut of
cows and buffaloes, and make methane,

39
00:01:41,291 --> 00:01:43,884
the gas in biogas, from their dung.

40
00:01:44,634 --> 00:01:48,501
Their secret is a cell wall built
differently from other bacteria.

41
00:01:48,501 --> 00:01:49,961
It helps them survive.

42
00:01:50,711 --> 00:01:52,712
Eubacteria means true bacteria.

43
00:01:52,712 --> 00:01:56,607
They have a rigid cell wall,
and a flagellum if they move.

44
00:01:57,357 --> 00:01:58,331
Your turn.

45
00:01:58,331 --> 00:02:01,610
A bacterium lives in a hot, acidic spring.

46
00:02:01,610 --> 00:02:03,714
Archaebacteria or eubacteria?

47
00:02:03,714 --> 00:02:05,146
Pause and decide.

48
00:02:08,346 --> 00:02:09,489
Archaebacteria!

49
00:02:09,489 --> 00:02:11,365
It is a thermoacidophile.

50
00:02:11,365 --> 00:02:14,751
Its special cell wall
lets it survive the heat.

51
00:02:15,401 --> 00:02:18,287
Now meet the eubacteria,
by how they get food.

52
00:02:18,637 --> 00:02:23,613
Cyanobacteria, also called blue green
algae, have chlorophyll a, like green

53
00:02:23,613 --> 00:02:24,513
plants.

54
00:02:24,303 --> 00:02:26,185
They make their own food.

55
00:02:26,935 --> 00:02:32,103
Some, like Nostoc and Anabaena, fix
nitrogen from the air in special cells

56
00:02:32,103 --> 00:02:33,545
called heterocysts.

57
00:02:34,295 --> 00:02:39,172
In polluted water, cyanobacteria
often grow into huge green blooms.

58
00:02:39,922 --> 00:02:43,229
Chemosynthetic bacteria
make food another way.

59
00:02:43,229 --> 00:02:48,355
They oxidise substances like nitrates,
nitrites and ammonia, and use the

60
00:02:48,355 --> 00:02:49,992
energy to make ATP.

61
00:02:50,742 --> 00:02:53,630
Heterotrophic bacteria
are the most common.

62
00:02:53,630 --> 00:02:58,778
They decompose dead things, set curd,
help make antibiotics, and fix nitrogen

63
00:02:58,778 --> 00:03:00,609
in the roots of legumes.

64
00:03:01,359 --> 00:03:05,846
Some cause disease, like cholera,
typhoid, tetanus, and citrus canker in

65
00:03:05,846 --> 00:03:06,746
plants.

66
00:03:07,125 --> 00:03:09,056
How do bacteria multiply?

67
00:03:09,056 --> 00:03:10,531
Mainly by fission.

68
00:03:10,531 --> 00:03:12,529
One cell splits into two.

69
00:03:12,529 --> 00:03:13,893
Two become four.

70
00:03:13,893 --> 00:03:15,369
Four become eight.

71
00:03:15,819 --> 00:03:18,112
In hard times, they form spores.

72
00:03:18,112 --> 00:03:22,726
They can also pass DNA from one cell
to another, a simple kind of sexual

73
00:03:22,726 --> 00:03:23,679
reproduction.

74
00:03:24,129 --> 00:03:26,757
And Mycoplasma have no cell wall at all.

75
00:03:26,757 --> 00:03:31,158
They are the smallest living cells
known, and can live without oxygen.

76
00:03:31,808 --> 00:03:32,885
Remember this.

77
00:03:32,885 --> 00:03:35,987
Every member of Monera
has no nuclear membrane.

78
00:03:36,587 --> 00:03:38,163
Now, a worked example.

79
00:03:38,163 --> 00:03:40,654
Let's place six bacteria in their groups.

80
00:03:41,004 --> 00:03:43,444
A methanogen in a buffalo's gut.

81
00:03:43,444 --> 00:03:44,642
Archaebacteria.

82
00:03:45,092 --> 00:03:47,576
Anabaena, with its heterocysts.

83
00:03:47,576 --> 00:03:48,748
Cyanobacteria.

84
00:03:49,198 --> 00:03:52,621
A bacterium that oxidises
ammonia for energy.

85
00:03:52,621 --> 00:03:53,864
Chemosynthetic.

86
00:03:54,314 --> 00:03:57,453
The curd bacterium, and
the typhoid bacterium.

87
00:03:57,453 --> 00:03:59,069
Both are heterotrophic.

88
00:03:59,569 --> 00:04:02,826
A cell with no wall at
all, the smallest known.

89
00:04:02,826 --> 00:04:03,726
Mycoplasma.

90
00:04:04,325 --> 00:04:05,791
Now, the exam trap.

91
00:04:06,141 --> 00:04:10,487
Many students think blue green algae
are algae, so they must be plants

92
00:04:10,487 --> 00:04:11,439
or protists.

93
00:04:11,839 --> 00:04:14,465
But blue green algae are cyanobacteria.

94
00:04:14,465 --> 00:04:18,189
Prokaryotes with no nuclear
membrane, in kingdom Monera.

95
00:04:18,839 --> 00:04:20,445
Where is this asked?

96
00:04:20,445 --> 00:04:25,293
As assertion and reason, or statements
on archaebacteria, heterocysts and

97
00:04:25,293 --> 00:04:26,220
Mycoplasma.

98
00:04:26,820 --> 00:04:28,090
Exam level now.

99
00:04:28,090 --> 00:04:30,578
Read the assertion and the reason.

100
00:04:30,578 --> 00:04:31,903
Pause and solve.

101
00:04:37,303 --> 00:04:38,624
The answer is B.

102
00:04:38,624 --> 00:04:40,406
Both statements are true.

103
00:04:40,906 --> 00:04:44,791
Cyanobacteria do have
chlorophyll a, like green plants.

104
00:04:45,291 --> 00:04:47,681
But that is not why they are in Monera.

105
00:04:47,681 --> 00:04:50,183
They are there because
they are prokaryotes.

106
00:04:50,784 --> 00:04:52,244
Here is your recap.

107
00:04:52,244 --> 00:04:53,745
Screenshot this card.

108
00:04:56,945 --> 00:04:59,782
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

109
00:05:00,182 --> 00:05:01,082
One.

110
00:05:00,709 --> 00:05:03,046
Name the four shapes of bacteria.

111
00:05:03,946 --> 00:05:04,846
Two.

112
00:05:04,422 --> 00:05:07,542
Where do methanogens live,
and what do they make?

113
00:05:08,442 --> 00:05:09,342
Three.

114
00:05:09,007 --> 00:05:11,779
What is a heterocyst, and what does it do?

115
00:05:12,879 --> 00:05:17,079
Next time: a drop of pond water
under the college microscope.

116
00:05:17,079 --> 00:05:18,454
What swims inside?

117
00:05:18,454 --> 00:05:20,740
We find out in kingdom Protista.
